 # Generative and Style-based Models
 
-Also called: Generative NeRF, Style-based Radiance Fields, 3D-aware GANs
+Also called: Generative NeRF, 3D-aware GANs, Latent-NeRF
 
 ## Overview
-Generative and Style-based Models extend the NeRF paradigm from reconstructing a single static scene to synthesizing novel, high-fidelity 3D-consistent images from a latent distribution. While standard NeRF optimizes a specific scene's geometry and appearance, these models learn a joint distribution over 3D scenes, enabling the generation of new instances (e.g., new faces, new cars) that are consistent across viewpoints. They distinguish themselves from sibling methods like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) or [Edit-NeRF](9388-edit-nerf.md) by treating the scene representation itself as a stochastic variable conditioned on a latent code, rather than optimizing a deterministic field for a fixed input dataset.
+Generative and Style-based Models extend the NeRF paradigm from reconstructing a single static scene to synthesizing novel, high-fidelity 3D-consistent images from a learned distribution of scenes [S1, S2]. Unlike standard NeRF which optimizes a single instance's weights, these methods train a generator network (often a GAN) to map latent codes to radiance fields, enabling the creation of new 3D objects or styles [S1, S2]. They distinguish themselves from sibling methods like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) or [Edit-NeRF](9388-edit-nerf.md) by focusing on unconditional or conditional generation from a dataset distribution rather than editing or conditioning on a specific input image [S1, S2].
 
